# PM document style preferences

> **Living rule.** When a new formatting pattern or preference is confirmed in conversation, add it here.

## General formatting

- **Tables over bullets** for any list with more than one attribute per item (FRs, NFRs, dependencies, open questions, user stories). Bullets are for single-attribute lists only.
- **Concise.** No long prose paragraphs inside sections. Short sentences; use a table or bullet if the content has structure.
- **No redundant headers.** Don't repeat section names in the section content.

## Standard table shapes

Use these column sets consistently:

| Section | Columns |
|---------|---------|
| Functional requirements (PRD §7) | `# \| Requirement \| Who needs this \| What it means \| Open Qs \| Covered by` |
| Non-functional requirements (PRD §8) | `# \| Requirement \| Notes \| Open Qs` |
| Dependencies (PRD §6) | `Dependency \| Owner \| Notes` |
| Success metrics (PRD §10) | `Release \| Requirements \| Acceptance criteria` |
| Open questions (PRD §12 / TRD §11) | `# \| Question \| Owner \| Status \| Answer` |
| TRD technical open questions (TRD §11) | `# \| Question \| Owner \| Status \| Blocks` |
| RACI (PRD §2) | `Name \| Role / Team \| Area of ownership \| RACI` |
| Goals (PRD §4) | `# \| Goal \| How it is measured` |
| Roadmap (PRD §11) | `Quarter \| Milestone \| Why this order \| FRs \| Key dependencies \| Target` |
| FR Coverage Audit (TRD §12) | `FR \| Requirement (summary) \| Covered by \| Status` |

## Open questions

- Tag architecture blockers as `[ARCH]` inline in the `#` column.
- Keep the full detail file at `docs/open-questions.md` (with Source and Date columns).
- In the PRD **§12**, embed the full table (all rows) — don't summarize into a separate themes table. Note in the §12 header that `[ARCH]` items are TRD-owned; untagged items are PRD/business-owned.
- Mirror `[ARCH]` items in the TRD **§11** with a `Blocks` column (which story/epic each question blocks).
- When an answer is given, update **Status → Resolved**, fill **Answer**, and update the relevant FR/NFR body.

## Leadership / status updates

- Post to the **project page** (the parent APC-XXXX page), not the Brief or PRD page.
- The project page is the one with `Sub-item` links pointing to the Brief and PRD — it is what leadership checks.
- Append as a new dated section (`## Project Latest Update — Week of [date]`) so history accumulates on the page.

### Leadership update content rules

**Include only what is relevant to project outcomes.** Leadership does not need internal document changes, story counts, or tooling updates.

| Section | Include | Exclude |
|---------|---------|---------|
| Progress | Meetings held, decisions made, working sessions completed, milestones achieved | PRD/TRD restructuring, RACI edits, Notion tooling, command files, story-level backlog changes |
| Focus next week | Architecture sessions to schedule, stakeholder meetings, key decisions to drive | Story grooming, document formatting, internal process tasks |
| Decisions needed | Leadership-level gates: EA assignment, scope confirmation, partner decisions | Story-level blockers, tooling choices, internal PM process |
| Risks | Business and delivery risks with mitigants | Internal document drift, tooling gaps |
| Links | Project page link only (optional) | Notion page links, ADO links, doc paths |

**Format:** Tables and bullets; no prose paragraphs; no story IDs or Notion URLs in the body.

## Sync workflow — review before push (REQUIRED)

**Never auto-push to Notion without explicit user confirmation.** Follow this sequence every time:

1. **Draft locally** — make all changes to the `.md` file first.
2. **Show a diff summary** — present a concise bulleted list of what changed (section, what was added/modified/removed). Do NOT push yet.
3. **Wait for approval** — user says "looks good", "push it", "send to Notion", or equivalent. If user asks to revise, update the local file and show the diff again.
4. **Push to Notion** — only then call `notion-update-page`. Push only the changed sections, not the full page unless necessary.
5. **Confirm sync** — report which Notion page(s) were updated and what sections changed.

> Exception: if the user explicitly says "push directly" or "don't ask", skip step 3.

## Sync trigger events

Push to Notion when any of these happen:
- An open question is answered → update `open-questions.md` + PRD §12 + TRD §11 + unblock the relevant FR/story
- A FR changes → review TRD §10 for affected stories; update both docs
- A story is groomed or accepted → update ADO ID in TRD §10; sync status
- Weekly leadership update is drafted
- Architecture decision is made → remove `[ARCH]` tag from FR, update story AC, update TRD §11

## Document hierarchy (source of truth order)

```
Meeting notes → Open Questions → PRD (§7 FRs) → TRD (§10 backlog) → ADO
```
- Changes flow **downward** only. ADO stories never update the PRD without a meeting/decision first.
- The local `.md` files are always the review/draft layer. Notion is the published layer.
- **PRD = what and why** (§1–§13): Overview, RACI, Business Problem, Goals, Scope, Dependencies, FRs, NFRs, Flows, Success Metrics, Roadmap, Open Questions, Appendix.
- **TRD = how** (§1–§13): Overview, Architecture, System Interactions, Data Mapping, APIs, Migration, Cutover, Technical Validation, Operational Support, Engineering Backlog, Open Questions (technical), FR Coverage Audit, ADO Connection.
- Both docs open with a cross-reference to each other. Shared terms (system names, flow IDs, FR IDs) must be identical across both.

## TRD (Technical Requirements)

The TRD is the companion doc to the PRD. It defines the technical design and implementation. It does **not** restate business context — it references the PRD.

### TRD section map

| § | Section | Status during discovery |
|---|---------|------------------------|
| 1 | Overview (technical summary, refs PRD) | Fill immediately |
| 2 | Architecture | Placeholder until EA working sessions complete |
| 3 | System Interactions | Placeholder — use flow ID table referencing PRD §9 |
| 4 | Data Mapping | Placeholder — gated on OQ F1, F2 |
| 5 | APIs / Interfaces | Placeholder — gated on [ARCH] resolution |
| 6 | Migration Mechanics | Placeholder — refs PRD §11 Roadmap |
| 7 | Cutover Approach | Placeholder |
| 8 | Technical Validation | Stub table (test type, FR/NFR, method, owner) |
| 9 | Operational Support Model | Placeholder |
| 10 | Engineering Backlog (master backlog + story details) | Active |
| 11 | Open Questions (Technical) | Active — [ARCH] items mirrored from PRD §12 with Blocks column |
| 12 | FR Coverage Audit | Active — every FR mapped to implementing stories |
| 13 | ADO Connection | Active |

### Epic header format

Each Epic section must open with a 2-column metadata table before any story content:

```markdown
| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Goal** | One sentence — what this epic delivers |
| **Linked FRs** | FR-1, FR-2 |
| **ADO Feature(s)** | [677038](https://axasurion.visualstudio.com/...) |
| **Quarter** | Q1 |
| **Status** | Discovery |
| **Architecture blockers** | A1, A2 — stories `[BLOCKED]` until resolved |
```

### Master backlog table (TRD §10)

Single flat table interleaving Epic and Story rows. Epic rows hold ADO Feature IDs; Story rows hold ADO User Story IDs once created.

```markdown
| Type | ID | Summary | FR | Quarter | Priority | Status | ADO ID | Blocked by |
|------|----|---------|----|---------|----------|--------|--------|------------|
| **Epic** | EP-1 | DAX availability + UBIF | FR-1, FR-2, FR-6 | Q1 | — | Discovery | 677038 · 679502 | A1 A2 D1–D4 |
| Story | EP1-S1 | Real-time availability endpoint | FR-1 | Q1 | P0 | Draft | _(pending)_ | A1, A2, D1, D2 |
```

- **Type** column: `**Epic**` (bold) or `Story`
- **FR** column: map each story to its specific FR(s), not just the epic-level list
- **ADO ID** column: Epic rows → ADO Feature link(s); Story rows → ADO User Story link once created
- Story detail blocks (As a / I want / So that + numbered AC) go in a `### Story details` section below the master table, grouped by epic

> **Future (grooming phase):** Convert to 3-table Notion relational database (FRs ↔ Epics ↔ Stories). See `PROJECT-CONTEXT.md` Phase gates.

### Story status lifecycle

`Draft` → `Grooming Ready` → `In ADO` → `In Sprint` → `Done`

- **Draft**: story written, blockers unresolved — do not groom
- **Grooming Ready**: every referenced [ARCH] blocker is resolved — ready to take to sprint planning
- **In ADO**: work item created in Azure DevOps; ADO Story ID column filled
- **In Sprint**: actively being developed
- **Done**: shipped and validated

**Gating rule:** A story must NOT move to `Grooming Ready` while any open question in its `Blocked by` column has Status = Open in `open-questions.md`.

### ADO sync rules for TRD

- When a User Story is created in ADO: fill the ADO Story ID column with a hyperlink; change Status → `In ADO`
- When a User Story moves to sprint: change Status → `In Sprint`
- When an [ARCH] blocker is resolved: update Blocked by column, remove from TRD §11, re-evaluate gating rule, move eligible stories to `Grooming Ready`

